  SELECT 'listening-p2-set015-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"A"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Improve work performance**\n\nAt three o''clock, my concentration used to disappear. I''d reread the same email, make silly mistakes, and reach for another coffee. A colleague suggested using half my lunch break for a brisk walk, which sounded like a waste of time. After a week, though, I noticed I was finishing reports earlier and handling difficult clients more calmly. I haven''t become a fitness enthusiast, but I protect that thirty-minute walk now because the rest of my working day goes so much better.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Find exercise tiring**\n\nMy sister finishes a workout glowing and cheerful. I finish one looking as though I''ve missed the last bus in a thunderstorm. Ten minutes into a class, my legs feel heavy; by the end, even climbing the stairs to my flat seems unreasonable. I keep trying because my doctor says movement is important, and I admit I sleep well afterwards. But that famous burst of energy everyone promises? It has never arrived. Exercise simply drains me.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Have fun exercising with others**\n\nLast month, I joined a dance-fitness class only because my neighbour refused to go alone. Neither of us could follow the instructor at first, and we laughed so much that the people behind us started laughing too. Now six of us meet before class, share music, and celebrate whenever someone finally learns a difficult routine. I rarely notice how hard we''re working until the session ends. The company, jokes, and friendly competition are what keep me coming back.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Hate exercising**\n\nPeople keep offering me new ways to \"discover the joy\" of exercise: swimming, cycling, yoga, even boxing. I''ve tried enough of them to know the problem isn''t the activity. I dislike changing into sports clothes, counting repetitions, sweating, and waking up with sore muscles. A walk to the bakery is fine because it has a useful destination, but exercising just for the sake of it feels like unpaid work. Give me a novel and a comfortable chair instead.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["342","343","344","345"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set015-q002' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"B"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Improve work performance**\n\nAt three o''clock, my concentration used to disappear. I''d reread the same email, make silly mistakes, and reach for another coffee. A colleague suggested using half my lunch break for a brisk walk, which sounded like a waste of time. After a week, though, I noticed I was finishing reports earlier and handling difficult clients more calmly. I haven''t become a fitness enthusiast, but I protect that thirty-minute walk now because the rest of my working day goes so much better.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Find exercise tiring**\n\nMy sister finishes a workout glowing and cheerful. I finish one looking as though I''ve missed the last bus in a thunderstorm. Ten minutes into a class, my legs feel heavy; by the end, even climbing the stairs to my flat seems unreasonable. I keep trying because my doctor says movement is important, and I admit I sleep well afterwards. But that famous burst of energy everyone promises? It has never arrived. Exercise simply drains me.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Have fun exercising with others**\n\nLast month, I joined a dance-fitness class only because my neighbour refused to go alone. Neither of us could follow the instructor at first, and we laughed so much that the people behind us started laughing too. Now six of us meet before class, share music, and celebrate whenever someone finally learns a difficult routine. I rarely notice how hard we''re working until the session ends. The company, jokes, and friendly competition are what keep me coming back.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Hate exercising**\n\nPeople keep offering me new ways to \"discover the joy\" of exercise: swimming, cycling, yoga, even boxing. I''ve tried enough of them to know the problem isn''t the activity. I dislike changing into sports clothes, counting repetitions, sweating, and waking up with sore muscles. A walk to the bakery is fine because it has a useful destination, but exercising just for the sake of it feels like unpaid work. Give me a novel and a comfortable chair instead.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["342","343","344","345"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
