  SELECT 'listening-p2-set017-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"A"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Doing as a social activity**\n\nOur neighbourhood mural started with one blank wall and a box of donated paint. On Saturday morning, children drew outlines, older residents suggested local landmarks, and a professional artist somehow fitted all the ideas together. I painted a terrible-looking tree, but nobody minded; someone turned it into part of a forest. By lunchtime, strangers were sharing brushes and sandwiches. The finished picture is lovely, yet the best part was watching people who had never spoken before create something side by side.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Doing arts with children**\n\nWhen I set up paints for my nieces, I used to demonstrate exactly what they should make. They ignored me, mixed every colour into purple, and turned the paper sideways halfway through. Their pictures were far more imaginative than my neat example. Now I simply cover the table, give them old shirts, and let them lead. We invent stories about each drawing while we work. Making art with them is messy, unpredictable, and easily the most entertaining part of my week.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Doing arts as part of the job**\n\nPeople imagine my design job is just sitting around waiting for inspiration. Yesterday, however, I redrew a restaurant logo seven times because the client wanted it \"friendlier but more serious.\" There are budgets, deadlines, meetings, and files that must be prepared perfectly for printing. I still enjoy choosing colours and turning a rough idea into a strong image, but it is not a private hobby. The artwork has to solve someone else''s problem and be delivered on time.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Doing arts alone**\n\nA friend invited me to a weekend painting group, but conversation breaks my concentration. I like shutting the spare-room door, putting my phone face down, and working until I forget what time it is. No one asks what the picture means or suggests a brighter colour. Some evenings I produce nothing worth keeping, and that is fine because nobody is watching. The privacy gives me room to experiment, make mistakes, and follow an idea without explaining it.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["350","351","352","353"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
