  SELECT 'listening-p2-set020-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"A"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Walk with friends**\n\nAt 7:40 each morning, three of us meet beside the bakery. The direct route to the office takes twenty minutes, although we often choose the riverside path and arrive five minutes later. One friend brings coffee, another gives us the latest neighbourhood news, and I mostly listen until I am properly awake. Rain occasionally sends us onto the bus, but walking together has become part of our friendship rather than simply a way of reaching work.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Go by bus**\n\nDriving would be faster before seven, but I am never organised enough to leave that early. By eight, the roads are blocked and parking near the office costs a fortune. The number 26 stops almost outside my flat, so I board with a travel card and read the news until my stop. It is not always comfortable-schoolchildren fill every seat on Mondays-but I can let someone else deal with the traffic.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Walk alone**\n\nMy colleague lives two streets away and keeps suggesting we travel together. The truth is that the forty-minute walk is the only part of my day. At home, two children need breakfast; at work, meetings begin the moment I enter. Between those worlds, I put my phone away and follow the canal path by myself. I sometimes greet dog walkers, but I do not arrange company. Solitude is the point of the journey.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Drive car**\n\nThe train station is closer than my garage, yet the train requires two changes and frequently leaves me standing in the rain. With my own vehicle, I can drop my daughter at school, carry equipment for work, and take a different road when traffic is bad. Fuel and parking are expensive, so I share the journey with a colleague twice a week. Even with those costs, having the car gives my complicated mornings the flexibility they need.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["362","363","364","365"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set020-q002' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"B"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Walk with friends**\n\nAt 7:40 each morning, three of us meet beside the bakery. The direct route to the office takes twenty minutes, although we often choose the riverside path and arrive five minutes later. One friend brings coffee, another gives us the latest neighbourhood news, and I mostly listen until I am properly awake. Rain occasionally sends us onto the bus, but walking together has become part of our friendship rather than simply a way of reaching work.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Go by bus**\n\nDriving would be faster before seven, but I am never organised enough to leave that early. By eight, the roads are blocked and parking near the office costs a fortune. The number 26 stops almost outside my flat, so I board with a travel card and read the news until my stop. It is not always comfortable-schoolchildren fill every seat on Mondays-but I can let someone else deal with the traffic.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Walk alone**\n\nMy colleague lives two streets away and keeps suggesting we travel together. The truth is that the forty-minute walk is the only part of my day. At home, two children need breakfast; at work, meetings begin the moment I enter. Between those worlds, I put my phone away and follow the canal path by myself. I sometimes greet dog walkers, but I do not arrange company. Solitude is the point of the journey.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Drive car**\n\nThe train station is closer than my garage, yet the train requires two changes and frequently leaves me standing in the rain. With my own vehicle, I can drop my daughter at school, carry equipment for work, and take a different road when traffic is bad. Fuel and parking are expensive, so I share the journey with a colleague twice a week. Even with those costs, having the car gives my complicated mornings the flexibility they need.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids"