  SELECT 'listening-p2-set026-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"A"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Does not use commercial cleaning products**\n\nThe supermarket cleaning aisle smells so strong that it makes my eyes water. Worse, every product comes in another plastic bottle. I began making one simple spray from vinegar, water, and citrus peel, expecting it to be a temporary experiment. It cleaned the kitchen surprisingly well, so I added baking soda for tougher jobs. I still buy soap, of course, but the specialised chemical cleaners have disappeared from my cupboard and from my shopping list.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Give away used items**\n\nMy wardrobe was full, yet I wore the same ten things. Rather than throw the rest away, I organised a free clothes table at our community centre. A teenager chose my old winter coat, and another family took bedding we no longer needed. They later brought toys for someone else to collect. Passing things on creates a useful circle: one person''s clutter becomes another person''s solution, with no new product needing to be made.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Buy environmentally friendly products**\n\nI cannot control how every factory operates, but I can decide which ones receive my money. Before buying, I now check whether packaging is recyclable, materials are responsibly sourced, and replacement parts are available. Last month I chose a repairable kettle over a cheaper sealed model. It may last longer and create less waste. Shopping this way takes more thought, but it turns an ordinary purchase into support for better production.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Reuse containers for storing food**\n\nSunday cooking once ended with a bin full of tubs and jars. I started washing them instead: yoghurt pots hold chopped herbs, glass jars store soup, and takeaway containers carry lunches to work. I label everything because frozen tomato sauce and strawberry puree look alarmingly similar. The system saves money and reduces single-use packaging. More importantly, it makes me pause before treating a perfectly useful container as rubbish.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["386","387","388","389"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set026-q002' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"B"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Does not use commercial cleaning products**\n\nThe supermarket cleaning aisle smells so strong that it makes my eyes water. Worse, every product comes in another plastic bottle. I began making one simple spray from vinegar, water, and citrus peel, expecting it to be a temporary experiment. It cleaned the kitchen surprisingly well, so I added baking soda for tougher jobs. I still buy soap, of course, but the specialised chemical cleaners have disappeared from my cupboard and from my shopping list.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Give away used items**\n\nMy wardrobe was full, yet I wore the same ten things. Rather than throw the rest away, I organised a free clothes table at our community centre. A teenager chose my old winter coat, and another family took bedding we no longer needed. They later brought toys for someone else to collect. Passing things on creates a useful circle: one person''s clutter becomes another person''s solution, with no new product needing to be made.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Buy environmentally friendly products**\n\nI cannot control how every factory operates, but I can decide which ones receive my money. Before buying, I now check whether packaging is recyclable, materials are responsibly sourced, and replacement parts are available. Last month I chose a repairable kettle over a cheaper sealed model. It may last longer and create less waste. Shopping this way takes more thought, but it turns an ordinary purchase into support for better production.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Reuse containers for storing food**\n\nSunday cooking once ended with a bin full of tubs and jars. I started washing them instead: yoghurt pots hold chopped herbs, glass jars store soup, and takeaway containers carry lunches to work. I label everything because frozen tomato sauce and strawberry puree look alarmingly similar. The system saves money and reduces single-use packaging. More importantly, it makes me pause before treating a perfectly useful container as rubbish.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["386","387","388","389"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set026-q003' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"C"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Does not use commercial cleaning products**\n\nThe supermarket cleaning aisle smells so strong that it makes my eyes water. Worse, every product comes in another plastic bottle. I began making one simple spray from vinegar, water, and citrus peel, expecting it to be a temporary experiment. It cleaned the kitchen surprisingly well, so I added baking soda for tougher jobs. I still buy soap, of course, but the specialised chemical cleaners have disappeared from my cupboard and from my shopping list.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Give away used items**\n\nMy wardrobe was full, yet I wore the same ten things. Rather than throw the rest away, I organised a free clothes table at our community centre. A teenager chose my old winter coat, and another family took bedding we no longer needed. They later brought toys for someone else to collect. Passing things on creates a useful circle: one person''s clutter becomes another person''s solution, with no new product needing to be made.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Buy environmentally friendly products**\n\nI cannot control how every factory operates, but I can decide which ones receive my money. Before buying, I now check whether packaging is recyclable, materials are responsibly sourced, and replacement parts are available. Last month I chose a repairable kettle over a cheaper sealed model. It may last longer and create less waste. Shopping this way takes more thought, but it turns an ordinary purchase into support for better production.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Reuse containers for storing food**\n\nSunday cooking once ended with a bin full of tubs and jars. I started washing them instead: yoghurt pots hold chopped herbs, glass jars store soup, and takeaway containers carry lunches to work. I label everything because frozen tomato sauce and strawberry puree look alarmingly similar. The system saves money and reduces single-use packaging. More importantly, it makes me pause before treating a perfectly useful container as rubbish.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["386","387","388","389"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set026-q004' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"D"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Does not use commercial cleaning products**\n\nThe supermarket cleaning aisle smells so strong that it makes my eyes water. Worse, every product comes in another plastic bottle. I began making one simple spray from vinegar, water, and citrus peel, expecting it to be a temporary experiment. It cleaned the kitchen surprisingly well, so I added baking soda for tougher jobs. I still buy soap, of course, but the specialised chemical cleaners have disappeared from my cupboard and from my shopping list.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Give away used items**\n\nMy wardrobe was full, yet I wore the same ten things. Rather than throw the rest away, I organised a free clothes table at our community centre. A teenager chose my old winter coat, and another family took bedding we no longer needed. They later brought toys for someone else to collect. Passing things on creates a useful circle: one person''s clutter becomes another person''s solution, with no new product needing to be made.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Buy environmentally friendly products**\n\nI cannot control how every factory operates, but I can decide which ones receive my money. Before buying, I now check whether packaging is recyclable, materials are responsibly sourced, and replacement parts are available. Last month I chose a repairable kettle over a cheaper sealed model. It may last longer and create less waste. Shopping this way takes more thought, but it turns an ordinary purchase into support for better production.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Reuse containers for storing food**\n\nSunday cooking once ended with a bin full of tubs and jars. I started washing them instead: yoghurt pots hold chopped herbs, glass jars store soup, and takeaway containers carry lunches to work. I label everything because frozen tomato sauce and strawberry puree look alarmingly similar. The system saves money and reduces single-use packaging. More importantly, it makes me pause before treating a perfectly useful container as rubbish.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["386","387","388","389"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set027-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"A"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Give away used items**\n\nWhen we moved, I counted six lamps, three chairs, and boxes of clothes we no longer used. Hiring a rubbish collection would have been easiest, but most of it was perfectly good. I posted photographs in a neighbourhood group, and by Sunday everything had new owners, including a student delighted with the old desk. Nothing earned me money, yet seeing useful objects leave in people''s hands rather than a truck felt far better.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Buy environmentally friendly products**\n\nI used to choose shampoo by smell and washing-up liquid by price. Then our local river was closed after pollution levels rose, and I started reading labels. Now I look for refillable bottles, recycled packaging, and ingredients that break down safely. These products sometimes cost a little more, so I buy fewer of them and use them carefully. My shopping basket is a small thing, but it supports companies making less damaging choices.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Reuse containers for storing food**\n\nMy grandmother never threw away a glass jar; I used to tease her cupboard full of them. Now my kitchen looks remarkably similar. Coffee jars hold rice, takeaway boxes carry lunches, and small containers organise herbs in the freezer. Reusing them means I buy fewer plastic bags and can actually see what food I have, so less gets forgotten. Grandmother calls it common sense, not environmentalism, and perhaps she is right.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Does not use commercial cleaning products**\n\nThe warning labels on bathroom cleaners began to bother me-open the window, wear gloves, keep away from children. I wondered why something used in my home needed so many hazards. After some experiments, I found that diluted vinegar handles glass, baking soda cleans the sink, and lemon removes unpleasant smells. I no longer bring home rows of brightly coloured chemical bottles. The homemade mixtures are simpler, cheaper, and much kinder to what goes down the drain.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["390","391","392","393"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set027-q002' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"B"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — Give away used items**\n\nWhen we moved, I counted six lamps, three chairs, and boxes of clothes we no longer used. Hiring a rubbish collection would have been easiest, but most of it was perfectly good. I posted photographs in a neighbourhood group, and by Sunday everything had new owners, including a student delighted with the old desk. Nothing earned me money, yet seeing useful objects leave in people''s hands rather than a truck felt far better.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — Buy environmentally friendly products**\n\nI used to choose shampoo by smell and washing-up liquid by price. Then our local river was closed after pollution levels rose, and I started reading labels. Now I look for refillable bottles, recycled packaging, and ingredients that break down safely. These products sometimes cost a little more, so I buy fewer of them and use them carefully. My shopping basket is a small thing, but it supports companies making less damaging choices.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — Reuse containers for storing food**\n\nMy grandmother never threw away a glass jar; I used to tease her cupboard full of them. Now my kitchen looks remarkably similar. Coffee jars hold rice, takeaway boxes carry lunches, and small containers organise herbs in the freezer. Reusing them means I buy fewer plastic bags and can actually see what food I have, so less gets forgotten. Grandmother calls it common sense, not environmentalism, and perhaps she is right.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — Does not use commercial cleaning products**\n\nThe warning labels on bathroom cleaners began to bother me-open the window, wear gloves, keep away from children. I wondered why something used in my home needed so many hazards. After some experiments, I found that diluted vinegar handles glass, baking soda cleans the sink, and lemon removes unpleasant smells. I no longer bring home rows of brightly coloured chemica