  SELECT 'listening-p2-set029-q004' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"D"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — To relax**\n\nMy train home is crowded, but headphones create a small private room around me. I choose slow music, look out of the window, and let conversations fade into the background. By my stop, the day''s problems feel less urgent. I cannot use music while reading because I follow the melody instead of the words, and loud morning songs irritate me. I save it for the moments when I need to unwind.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — After waking up**\n\nBefore I discovered morning playlists, I pressed the snooze button three or four times. Now the speaker starts with a song I love at seven, and I have to cross the room to turn it up. I dance badly while making coffee, which is ridiculous but effective. At other times of day I may prefer silence; immediately after sleep, however, music does the job that alarms and motivation never managed.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — While singing**\n\nOur family karaoke machine was meant for parties, but I use it nearly every evening. I play the original track once, then remove the lead vocal and try the song myself. Sometimes I send a recording to my choir teacher for feedback. Background music while working only distracts me, so I do not keep it on casually. I listen closely when I am about to sing, because the song becomes a lesson.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — While studying**\n\nA silent study room makes me aware of every page turning. A crowded room is worse. Soft electronic music through headphones sits between those extremes and creates a steady wall of sound. I avoid familiar songs because memories come with them, and I never use shuffle-the sudden change breaks my focus. That carefully chosen music accompanies essays, revision, and almost every long assignment I complete.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["398","399","400","401"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::js