  SELECT 'listening-p2-set030-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"A"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — horse riding**\n\nThe horse I ride is called Pepper, and she knows the route better than I do. We leave the stable, cross a shallow stream, and follow a path through the fields. Cycling is faster and hiking is easier to organise, but neither involves learning to communicate with an animal that has its own mood and opinions. That partnership is what keeps me returning to the riding centre every weekend.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — going for a run**\n\nMy job keeps me indoors under artificial light, so after work I need movement and open sky. I lace up beside the office and follow a five-kilometre route around the lake. Some days I run ; on others I slow down and watch the birds. I tried joining a gym, but staring at a wall while exercising felt wrong. Running outside clears my head in a way no machine has managed.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — hiking**\n\nAfter my mountain-bike accident, I was reluctant to return to steep trails. I still missed the views, though, so a friend suggested reaching them on foot. Walking uphill takes longer and lacks the rush of cycling, but I notice wildflowers, old paths, and places I used to race past. The mountains are still part of my weekends; I simply explore them now with boots and a backpack.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — mountain biking**\n\nHorse riding looks elegant until a large horse turns its head toward me; then I remember how nervous animals make me. My friends and I prefer machines we can repair ourselves. We load our bikes onto a van, climb forest tracks, and race down muddy slopes. It is demanding, occasionally frightening, and guaranteed to cover us in dirt. That combination is exactly why mountain biking appeals to me.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["402","403","404","405"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set030-q002' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"B"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — horse riding**\n\nThe horse I ride is called Pepper, and she knows the route better than I do. We leave the stable, cross a shallow stream, and follow a path through the fields. Cycling is faster and hiking is easier to organise, but neither involves learning to communicate with an animal that has its own mood and opinions. That partnership is what keeps me returning to the riding centre every weekend.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — going for a run**\n\nMy job keeps me indoors under artificial light, so after work I need movement and open sky. I lace up beside the office and follow a five-kilometre route around the lake. Some days I run ; on others I slow down and watch the birds. I tried joining a gym, but staring at a wall while exercising felt wrong. Running outside clears my head in a way no machine has managed.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — hiking**\n\nAfter my mountain-bike accident, I was reluctant to return to steep trails. I still missed the views, though, so a friend suggested reaching them on foot. Walking uphill takes longer and lacks the rush of cycling, but I notice wildflowers, old paths, and places I used to race past. The mountains are still part of my weekends; I simply explore them now with boots and a backpack.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — mountain biking**\n\nHorse riding looks elegant until a large horse turns its head toward me; then I remember how nervous animals make me. My friends and I prefer machines we can repair ourselves. We load our bikes onto a van, climb forest tracks, and race down muddy slopes. It is demanding, occasionally frightening, and guaranteed to cover us in dirt. That combination is exactly why mountain biking appeals to me.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["402","403","404","405"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
