  SELECT 'listening-p2-set034-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"A"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — social activity**\n\nI joined a printmaking workshop without knowing anyone. By the end of the first evening, four of us were sharing ink, comparing mistakes, and planning a small exhibition in the café next door. The prints were individual, but the process was wonderfully communal; one person''s failed pattern often inspired another person''s idea. I enjoy the objects we produce, yet the real attraction is having a reason to gather, talk, and make something with other people.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — with children**\n\nChildren never ask whether blue trees are realistic. They paint them, add orange rain, and explain an entire world before an adult has chosen a pencil. I volunteer at a Saturday club, laying out materials and helping with scissors, but I try not to control the result. Their confidence reminds me to take art less seriously. Working beside them is noisy and chaotic, and it constantly refreshes my own imagination.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — as part of their job**\n\nEvery chapter of the educational book I edit needs illustrations. I sketch diagrams, discuss changes with the writer, and adjust colours so they remain clear when printed. Sometimes I would rather draw whatever interests me, but the image must support the text and meet a deadline. Art is woven into my working week, complete with feedback and revisions. It is creative, certainly, but it also has a practical purpose and a client.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — alone**\n\nGroup workshops make me compare every line with the person beside me. At home, that pressure disappears. I put on an old shirt, clear the kitchen table, and draw for hours without showing anyone. Some pages are ugly experiments that go straight into a drawer. That secrecy is valuable: I can change direction, abandon an idea, or make something deeply personal without an audience waiting for an explanation.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["418","419","420","421"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p2-set034-q002' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"B"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — social activity**\n\nI joined a printmaking workshop without knowing anyone. By the end of the first evening, four of us were sharing ink, comparing mistakes, and planning a small exhibition in the café next door. The prints were individual, but the process was wonderfully communal; one person''s failed pattern often inspired another person''s idea. I enjoy the objects we produce, yet the real attraction is having a reason to gather, talk, and make something with other people.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — with children**\n\nChildren never ask whether blue trees are realistic. They paint them, add orange rain, and explain an entire world before an adult has chosen a pencil. I volunteer at a Saturday club, laying out materials and helping with scissors, but I try not to control the result. Their confidence reminds me to take art less seriously. Working beside them is noisy and chaotic, and it constantly refreshes my own imagination.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — as part of their job**\n\nEvery chapter of the educational book I edit needs illustrations. I sketch diagrams, discuss changes with the writer, and adjust colours so they remain clear when printed. Sometimes I would rather draw whatever interests me, but the image must support the text and meet a deadline. Art is woven into my working week, complete with feedback and revisions. It is creative, certainly, but it also has a practical purpose and a client.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — alone**\n\nGroup workshops make me compare every line with the person beside me. At home, that pressure disappears. I put on an old shirt, clear the kitchen table, and draw for hours without showing anyone. Some pages are ugly experiments that go straight into a drawer. That secrecy is valuable: I can change direction, abandon an idea, or make something deeply personal without an audience waiting for an explanation.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["418","419","420","421"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","Person C","Person D"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
