  SELECT 'listening-p2-set040-q004' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"D"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"**Segment 1 — plays in a team sport**\n\nA solo run depends entirely on my own motivation, which is unreliable on cold mornings. Basketball is different. If I miss training, four teammates notice immediately. During a match, every decision connects to someone else: I pass, screen, defend, and trust another player to cover space. Winning feels shared, and even losing gives us something to analyse together. That cooperation is the reason team sport suits me.\n\n\n\n**Segment 2 — spends time in the water**\n\nOn land I am clumsy, but in a pool everything feels smoother. I swim before breakfast three times a week, counting lengths until the lifeguard changes shifts. Summer adds trips to the lake, where I can stay in for hours. Friends invite me to football and running, yet I never enjoy the heat or impact. Give me water, goggles, and a clear lane, and I am completely at home.\n\n\n\n**Segment 3 — plays in the street**\n\nOur neighbourhood had no sports ground, so two drain covers became goalposts and parked cars became the boundaries. We learned to stop the ball whenever a vehicle appeared and continued the match immediately afterwards. I now play at a proper club, but Sunday games still happen outside my parents'' house with whoever is available. The street is where I first learned football and where it still feels most joyful.\n\n\n\n**Segment 4 — does sports with animals**\n\nRiding is not simply sitting while the horse does the work. My legs, balance, and timing are active every second, and the animal responds to tiny changes I make. Building trust with a nervous horse can take weeks, which makes progress especially rewarding. I enjoy many outdoor sports, but none asks me to cooperate with another living creature in the same way.","transcript_status":"source_word_comments","word_comment_ids":["442","443","444","445"],"speaker_labels":["Person A","Person B","