  SELECT 'listening-p3-set016-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"both"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"M: I''ve been thinking about how children and teenagers use technology these days. Many of them seem really interested in learning through computers, tablets, and educational apps. Digital tools can make lessons feel more active and enjoyable.\nW: I agree. Students often become more motivated when technology is used in class. Interactive games, videos, and online exercises can make learning more exciting for them.\nM: However, I do worry that too much technology can affect their communication skills. Some children are comfortable sending messages online, but they find it harder to speak to people face to face.\nW: Yes, I''ve noticed that too. Some young people can write online, but when they have to talk directly to classmates or adults, they may feel nervous or unsure.\nW: Another problem is that many families don''t really know how to manage children''s technology use at home. Parents may not know how long children should spend online or what kinds of websites are suitable. I think families need more guidance.\nM: I''m not sure families always need outside help. I think children themselves can learn to control their online behaviour if they are taught properly. With the right education, they can learn when to stop using screens and how to communicate responsibly online. **Explanation summary** *Both speakers say children are interested in learning through technology and can struggle with face-to-face communication. The woman says families need more guidance to manage children''s technology use at home, while the man says children can learn to regulate their own online behaviour responsibly.*","transcript_status":"source_word_comment","word_comment_ids":["476"],"speaker_labels":["Man","Woman"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
