  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p3-set027-q001' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"woman"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"M: The documentary crew wants to film an urban farm on the abandoned railway yard. From the bridge, the rows of vegetables already make the area look less neglected.\nW: It is pleasant, but I still believe housing space matters more. The waiting list for homes is growing, and the yard is one of the few large sites left.\nM: Only part of it is suitable for building. The contaminated section can support raised beds, public paths, and trees.\nW: In that case, I agree the farm could make the district more attractive. It would replace fences and rubbish with a space people can actually use.\nM: It may also help the local economy. The project plans to train residents, supply street markets, and rent stalls to small food businesses.\nW: I am not convinced those jobs and stalls would last, and nobody should claim the project can meet the city''s food demand.\nM: Certainly not. Urban farming cannot solve shortages by itself.\nW: We agree there. It should complement rural producers and supermarkets, not replace them.\nM: That distinction would make a good ending for the documentary.\nW: Especially if the film also shows the housing debate rather than presenting a perfect green solution. **Explanation summary** *User-provided Part 3 topic version for Urban Farming ( version 3 ). Check the statement-level explanations for the final speaker mapping.*","transcript_status":"source_word_comment","word_comment_ids":["487"],"speaker_labels":["Man","Woman"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'listening-p3-set027-q002' AS sk, '{"correct_option":"both"}'::jsonb AS correct_answer, '{"transcript":"M: The documentary crew wants to film an urban farm on the abandoned railway yard. From the bridge, the rows of vegetables already make the area look less neglected.\nW: It is pleasant, but I still believe housing space matters more. The waiting list for homes is growing, and the yard is one of the few large sites left.\nM: Only part of it is suitable for building. The contaminated section can support raised beds, public paths, and trees.\nW: In that case, I agree the farm could make the district more attractive. It would replace fences and rubbish with a space people can actually use.\nM: It may also help the local economy. The project plans to train residents, supply street markets, and rent stalls to small food businesses.\nW: I am not convinced those jobs and stalls would last, and nobody should claim the project can meet the city''s food demand.\nM: Certainly not. Urban farming cannot solve shortages by itself.\nW: We agree there. It should complement rural producers and supermarkets, not replace them.\nM: That distinction would make a good ending for the documentary.\nW: Especially if the film also shows the housing debate rather than presenting a perfect green solution. **Explanation summary** *User-provided Part 3 topic version for Urban Farming ( version 3 ). Check the statement-level explanations for the final speaker mapping.*","transcript_status":"source_word_comment","word_comment_ids":["487"],"speaker_labels":["Man","Woman"]}'::jsonb AS solution_data
  UNION ALL
